{% failure-mode-option label="Partial or ambiguous result" symptom="The tool returns HTTP 200, but the payload is empty, incomplete, or in an unexpected shape." strategy="Validate the result shape the same way you validate arguments. Treat an incomplete result as a classification failure, not a silent success." code="return result.results.length > 0 ? { status: 'success', data: result } : { status: 'empty' };" /%}
{% /failure-mode-explorer %}

## What to log and trace for debugging

A tool call that fails once in staging is a bug report. A tool call that fails once a week in production, for one customer, on one input class, is a mystery unless the trace carries enough context to reconstruct it. Record these fields on every attempt, success or failure:

| Field | Why it matters |
| --- | --- |
| Tool name and schema version | Distinguishes a code regression from a model or prompt regression |
| Idempotency key | Lets you confirm a retry did or did not duplicate a side effect |
| Attempt number and elapsed time | Separates a slow dependency from a hung one |
| Raw arguments (redacted) | The only way to tell a malformed call from a hallucinated one after the fact |
| Classification (success / retryable / terminal) | Turns a log line into an actionable signal instead of a status code |
| Circuit-breaker state at call time | Explains a fast failure that would otherwise look like a missing timeout |

![A decision map for a failed tool call: idempotent transient failures retry with backoff, non-idempotent transient failures require an idempotency key, and permanent failures or an open circuit route to a fallback](/media/tool-calling-reliability-decision.svg)

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Diagnostic shortcut" title="Read the classification before the stack trace." body="When a tool-calling incident comes in, look at the recorded classification field first: schema_mismatch, unknown_tool, retryable_error, or terminal_error. It usually narrows the investigation to one seam before you open a single log line of raw output." tone="dark" /%}

Do not log the full prompt or unredacted arguments by default. A spike in `schema_mismatch` errors after a deploy is a useful, safe-to-share signal; the raw customer data inside a failed argument payload usually is not. Keep the redaction rule as strict for tool-call traces as for any other production log, and store the raw value only behind an access-controlled, short-retention path for authorized debugging. This connects directly to the broader practice in [debugging AI agents in production](/blog/debug-ai-agents-in-production): a trace is only useful if a person who was not in the room can read it and understand what happened.

### A worked example

Consider a support agent that calls `refund_order`. A customer reports the refund never appeared. The trace shows three attempts: the first timed out at 8 seconds, the second returned an HTTP 409 ("refund already in progress"), and the third succeeded. Without the idempotency key and the classification field, this looks like three separate refund attempts and raises the question of a possible double refund. With them, it is a five-second story: the first attempt likely succeeded server-side after the client-side timeout, the second attempt correctly detected the in-flight duplicate and returned a safe conflict, and the third attempt's "success" was actually a confirmation of the original transaction rather than a second charge. {% highlight %}That distinction — one real side effect versus three — is only recoverable if the idempotency key was generated once per logical action and threaded through every retry.{% /highlight %}

## Test the failure paths, not just the happy path

A tool-calling test suite that only exercises successful calls will not catch the bugs that show up in production. Build fixtures for each seam explicitly: invalid JSON, a schema-valid but semantically wrong argument, an unknown tool name, a simulated timeout, each HTTP status class, and a 200 response with an empty or malformed body. Run these against the real validation and retry code, not a mocked-out version of it, so a change to the schema or the backoff curve gets caught before it reaches a user. [AI agent testing](/blog/ai-agent-testing-unit-tests-production-evals) covers building this kind of fixture set as part of a broader evaluation practice; tool-call failure fixtures are one of the highest-value places to start because they are deterministic and cheap to assert against.

Review the failure fixtures for coverage the same way you would review test coverage for any other critical path: does every branch in the retry classifier have at least one fixture that exercises it? Does the circuit breaker have a fixture that opens it and a fixture that confirms the half-open probe? A validation layer that has never seen a malformed call in CI will meet its first one in production.

## Release checklist

- Every tool has a registered schema, and calls to unregistered tools are rejected, not fuzzy-matched.
- Every tool has an explicit timeout sized to its expected latency, not a shared global default.
- Non-idempotent tools require an idempotency key before a retry is allowed.
- Retries are bounded by attempt count and use backoff with jitter, not a fixed interval.
- A circuit breaker exists per tool, with a cooldown and a half-open probe.
- Fallback behavior is defined for at least the highest-traffic and highest-risk tools.
- Every attempt is logged with tool name, classification, attempt number, and elapsed time, with arguments redacted by default.
- Failure-path fixtures exist for malformed arguments, unknown tools, timeouts, each HTTP status class, and ambiguous results.

> **Decision rule:** If a tool call cannot be validated, bounded, and classified, it is not ready to run unattended — put a human in the loop until it can be.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Should every tool call be retried automatically?" answer="No. Only retry a call classified as a transient failure, and only automatically for idempotent tools. For non-idempotent tools, retry automatically only when an idempotency key confirms the prior attempt did not already take effect; otherwise stop and escalate." /%}

{% faq-item question="How many retry attempts is reasonable?" answer="Two to four total attempts is typical for interactive agents, with exponential backoff and jitter between them. More than that usually adds latency without meaningfully improving the odds of success, and can mask a dependency outage that a circuit breaker should be catching instead." /%}

{% faq-item question="What is the difference between a retry and a circuit breaker?" answer="A retry protects one call: it tries the same operation again after a transient failure. A circuit breaker protects the system: it tracks failures for a tool over time and stops sending new calls once a threshold is crossed, so a degraded dependency cannot slow down every concurrent request while it recovers." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can schema validation catch a hallucinated argument value?" answer="Only if the value's shape is wrong. Schema validation cannot tell that a syntactically valid account ID or date was fabricated rather than grounded in a prior tool result. Catching that requires tracking which values are known-valid from earlier steps and checking new arguments against that provenance, not just their type." /%}

{% faq-item question="What should the model see when a tool call fails?" answer="A structured, typed failure result — not a raw exception and not silence. Include the classification (validation error, timeout, unavailable) and enough detail for the model to decide whether to retry with corrected arguments, explain the failure to the user, or stop. Avoid returning free-text errors that invite the model to guess at a workaround." /%}
